{% sec 1, "When the Main Tool Hits Zero", "Why would a central bank buy bonds?", "When rates hit zero" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>In late 2008 and again in 2020, central banks cut their rates almost to zero and the economy still needed more help. You can't easily take rates much below zero: people would rather keep cash in a safe than pay a bank to hold it. So central banks reached for a second dial.</p>
    <p>Picture a market stall selling apples. If one enormous customer turns up and starts buying apples every day, the price of apples goes up. {% gl "QE" %}Quantitative easing{% endgl %} (QE) makes the central bank that enormous customer in the bond market. It buys huge amounts of government bonds (and sometimes other bonds), which pushes their prices up. And as Bond Prices and Yields showed, when a bond's price goes up its yield goes down.</p>
    <p>The policy rate controls the cost of borrowing for one night. QE reaches further: it pushes down the yields on 5-, 10- and 30-year bonds, which set the cost of mortgages, company borrowing and government borrowing for years ahead. The opposite, {% gl "quantitative tightening" %}quantitative tightening{% endgl %} (QT), shrinks the pile of bonds again.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The policy rate steers the short end of the yield curve; QE and QT steer the long end, by changing how many bonds private investors have to hold.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "How QE Works, Step by Step", "Where does the money come from, and where does it go?", "How QE works" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The key is a central bank's {% gl "central bank balance sheet" %}balance sheet{% endgl %}: a list of what it owns (assets, like the bonds it buys) and what it owes (liabilities, like the {% gl "reserves" %}reserves{% endgl %} banks keep with it). QE grows both sides by the same amount. Step through it with £100m:</p>
  </div>
