    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Follow the money</h3><p>Choose QE or QT, then step through who holds what. The numbers show each player's holdings in £ millions; coloured boxes and arrows show what moves at each step. Items marked * are owed to someone else: the central bank owes reserves to banks, and a bank owes deposits to its customers.</p></div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="presets qe-pick" id="fm-mode" aria-label="QE or QT"></div>
      <div class="presets qe-pick" id="fm-step" aria-label="Step"></div>
    </div>
    <svg class="chart" id="chart-fm" viewBox="0 0 640 190" role="img" aria-label="Boxes for the central bank, a commercial bank, a pension fund and other markets, showing gilts, reserves and deposits moving between them during QE or QT"></svg>
    <div class="qe-nav"><button type="button" id="fm-prev">← Back</button><button type="button" id="fm-next">Next step →</button></div>
    <div class="qe-text" id="fm-text" aria-live="polite"></div>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The three ways it works</h3>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Fewer long bonds for everyone else.<span class="why">When the central bank holds a big share of long-dated bonds, investors are left holding fewer of them and will accept a lower yield. Economists call it the {% gl "portfolio balance effect" %}portfolio balance effect{% endgl %}: sellers move into other assets, pushing their prices up too.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>A promise in action.<span class="why">A central bank buying billions of bonds is unlikely to raise rates soon. QE backs up forward guidance with money.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>Keeping markets working.<span class="why">In a panic (2008, March 2020, the UK pension crisis of 2022), nobody wants to buy, and prices can collapse for no good reason. A central bank that steps in as buyer stops the spiral.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Is QE Printing Money?", "Does QE cause inflation?", "Printing money?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>People often say QE is "printing money". It does create new money, but not in the way the phrase suggests.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>No notes are printed.</strong> The new money is reserves: electronic balances that only banks hold at the central bank. They can't be spent in a shop.</li>
      <li><strong>Swap, not gift.</strong> The seller gives up a bond and gets money. Their wealth hasn't changed; only its form has.</li>
      <li><strong>It's meant to be reversed.</strong> QT removes the reserves again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>After 2008, many people warned QE would cause high inflation. For the next decade inflation mostly stayed <em>below</em> 2%: the new reserves largely sat in the banking system while banks repaired themselves and households paid down debt. The 2020–21 round was different: it came alongside huge government spending, closed supply chains and then an energy shock, and inflation surged. Economists still argue about how much QE contributed. Most agree it mattered more when the money reached people who spent it.</p>
    <p>QE also had side effects. By pushing up the prices of bonds, shares and houses, it helped people who already owned assets more than people who didn't. And when rates rose after 2022, central banks were paying more interest on all those reserves than they earned on their bonds, so they made losses (in the UK, the Treasury covers them).</p>
    <h3>When it really is money printing: Nigeria</h3>
    <p>What QE is not is a central bank paying the government's bills. That's {% gl "monetary financing" %}monetary financing{% endgl %}. It's banned by treaty in the euro area, and other independent central banks avoid it (the Bank of England briefly extended the UK government's overdraft in 2020, and it was repaid within months). In Nigeria, the CBN's "ways and means" lending to the government grew to about ₦22.7 trillion by 2023, far above the legal limit of \(5\%\) of the previous year's revenue, before it was turned into long-term bonds. That money went straight into government spending, which is a big part of why inflation and the naira's fall were so severe. Ending it has been central to the CBN rebuilding trust since 2023.</p>
    <p>The CBN mostly does the opposite of QE: it sells its own short-term bills (OMO bills) to soak up spare naira, and keeps a high cash reserve requirement, both of which drain money from the banking system.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

